AB  - This chapter explores the journey from local model development to production-scale distributed training using Kubeflow Training Operators. Beginning with single-machine prototyping on laptops, we progressively scale through single-node GPU training to multinode distributed systems capable of training large language models across clusters with multiple GPUs. The chapter demonstrates how the same training code evolves through these phases with minimal modifications—primarily configuration changes rather than algorithmic rewrites. We provide comprehensive coverage of both Training Operator V1’s framework-specific architecture (TFJob, PyTorchJob) and V2’s unified TrainJob API with pluggable runtime support. Through practical examples, including BERT fine-tuning and custom distributed training implementations, readers learn to configure data parallelism, gradient synchronization strategies, and communication backends. The chapter includes detailed architectural diagrams illustrating operator patterns, custom resource definitions, and pod orchestration within Kubernetes clusters. Real-world deployment patterns are demonstrated through complete code examples, including Python training scripts, Kubernetes manifests, and TensorBoard integration for monitoring convergence metrics. We address critical operational concerns such as GPU resource management, network communication patterns (AllReduce, Ring-AllReduce), checkpoint strategies, and debugging distributed training failures. By the chapter’s end, readers will understand how to leverage Kubeflow Training Operators to build scalable, reproducible machine learning training infrastructure that transitions seamlessly from experimental development to production deployment.
