KW  - Railway intrusion
KW  - Event-level perception
KW  - Non-specific foreign objects
KW  - Spatiotemporal reasoning
AB  - Foreign object intrusion is a persistent threat to railway operational safety, yet the diversity and unpredictability of real intruders make their detection and early warning particularly difficult. Closed-set detectors fail on objects outside their predefined categories, while recent multimodal foundation models, despite their open-set generalization, hallucinate on weak visual evidence of small intruders and lack the spatial reasoning needed to relate an intruder to the track geometry. In addition, existing detection paradigms tend to produce per-frame recognition results, leaving human operators to reconstruct the whole intrusion event on their own. This paper proposes an event-level perception framework that addresses these limitations without model training or parameter updates. Concretely, we decouple class-agnostic localization from semantic recognition, so that intruders can be detected without extracting class-specific features. A normalized track-relative coordinate system is then constructed, converting pixel-domain perception into a symbolic, geometry-aware representation that a language model can reason over directly. Building on this representation, a chain-of-thought reasoning stage consolidates noisy per-frame evidence into a structured event description. Experiments on a field-collected railway intrusion dataset show that the framework is robust across object categories. The trajectory estimates remain more accurate than category predictions for visually ambiguous targets, supporting the proposed localization–recognition separation and spatial representation. The framework also improves over end-to-end vision–language model (VLM), open-vocabulary, and closed-set baselines on most long-tail intruder categories while producing event-level outputs that detector-only baselines do not support.
ER  - 

