KW  - Multi-Label recognition
KW  - Graph representation learning
KW  - Domain knowledge
KW  - Tongue image
AB  - Tongue images have been proven to be clearly associated with a variety of diseases, and accurate multi-label recognition is key to tongue image analysis. Current methods for tongue image multi-label recognition consider only the information in the image, ignoring the value of tongue image domain knowledge in multi-label recognition. To overcome these challenges, we introduce a domain knowledge-guided two-stream graph representation (DKG-TSGR) framework that employs domain knowledge to guide adaptive information propagation across categories. The framework comprises two key modules: a semantic stream and a spatial stream. In the semantic stream, an in-context learning is developed to integrate inherent knowledge from the large language models, followed by the learning of more discriminative category semantics within the class-label hierarchy and the employment of a graph propagation network to capture contextualized category semantics. In the spatial stream, category semantics from the semantic stream are introduced to guide the learning of semantic-specific features for graph initialization, and a second graph is employed to propagate node messages. Multi-label recognition is facilitated by the joint exploration of feature and semantic spaces, especially for tongue image data with visually similar categories. Extensive experiments on two public tongue image multi-label datasets demonstrate that the proposed DKG-TSGR framework outperforms eight existing multi-label recognition methods in key metrics, including mAP, F1 score, and EMR.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
T1  - The promises and pitfalls of the use of artificial intelligence and large language model tools in qualitative surgical research
AU  - Szczygiel, Lauren A.
JO  - Surgery
VL  - 199
SP  - 110506
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/11/01/
SN  - 0039-6060
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.surg.2026.110506
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0039606026004319
AB  - Background
To examine how artificial intelligence and large language model tools may support qualitative surgical research, where they may threaten rigor and trustworthiness, and what principles should guide their responsible use.
Methods
This perspective reviews potential applications of artificial intelligence and large language model tools across the qualitative research process and considers their implications for analytic rigor, trustworthiness, and ethical practice.
Results
Artificial intelligence and large language model tools may be useful for bounded tasks, such as transcript summarization, data organization, preliminary code suggestion, and excerpt retrieval. However, these tools may also flatten nuance, generate hallucinated or misleading outputs, obscure interpretive processes through black-box functioning, reproduce training-data biases, and reduce researcher immersion in the data. Their responsible use depends on human oversight, verification against source data, and transparent reporting.
Conclusion
Artificial intelligence and large language model tools may be most useful when applied to bounded supportive tasks rather than as substitutes for researcher interpretation, reflexivity, or analytic judgment.
ER  - 

