AB  - This study examines how different dimensions of social capital shape individuals' perceptions of environmental health risks. While prior research primarily relies on survey-based measures capturing networks, trust, and participation, such approaches often overlook the affective and emotional aspects of social capital. We introduce an approach that leverages text-based measures to capture individuals’ emotional attachment to their communities. Using survey data collected in Texas (2023-24), we employ multiple text analysis tools (including the use of LLM) to demonstrate the validity of using textual data as an indicator of individual social capital. Then, we combine the new text-based score measure with traditional survey indices and estimate their relationship with perceived health risks from environmental hazards. Our results assessing specific health risks like respiratory disease and mental health conditions reveal contrasting effects: text-based social capital is positively associated with higher risk perceptions, whereas survey-based measures are negatively associated with perceived risks. These findings highlight the multidimensional nature of social capital and demonstrate the value of integrating diverse measurement approaches to better understand public responses to environmental hazards.
