AB  - Complex systems are ubiquitous across science and society, encompassing diverse domains ranging from conservation (endangered species) to blockchain (Ethereum ecosystem). These systems typically have multiple key factors with complex dependencies and interactions, several data sources of varying quality, and inherent uncertainty. Such a blend of characteristics challenge traditional modelling approaches. There are several modelling techniques available to model complex systems, and Bayesian network (BN) modelling has emerged as a particularly powerful approach for representing complex systems. BNs capture probabilistic relationships between factors and enable reasoning under uncertainty. However, their interpretation, and application often require specialised expertise that can limit their accessibility and relevance for the very people they were designed for. This is exacerbated when the team that developed and implemented the BN model, is no longer available to provide technical expertise, perhaps because they have since been assigned to another project. We demonstrate how artificial intelligence (AI) can be employed to assist in the accessibility and interpretability of complex models, and in so doing extend the useful lifetime of these models. Our overarching objective is to remove, or lower, the barrier of access to complex models, enabling users to interact with, and most importantly run, complex models such as BNs , by simply engaging with the model in conversational language, and additionally offering the user alternative ways to interact with the complex system model.
