AB  - Training for the United Nations Early Warnings for All initiative draws on guidance spread across publications of the World Meteorological Organization and the United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction, and it serves multilingual trainees for whom an answer without a verifiable source can mislead warning practice. We present a knowledge service in which one expert-reviewed concept record is the unit that is retrieved, cited and reviewed, and every record is bound to a publication, chapter and page. A large language model answers only from the retrieved records, answers only the supported part of a partly answerable request, declines when the records lack the requested fact, and calls bounded graph tools for multi-concept questions. Over five runs on 184 questions in Chinese, English and Arabic, three blinded language-model judges rated 99.3% of the single-turn service's answers to answerable questions correct, and it declined all 105 judged domain-relevant requests that its sources could not support. It exceeded hybrid and iterative retrieval over raw text in answer correctness, with the widest margins on Arabic and multi-concept questions, at about half and a quarter of their tokens. Given no evidence, the same model, although instructed to decline, invented specifics such as local thresholds in 18 of 24 unsupported requests. The service has supported two international courses with 69 trainees from 39 countries and regions and an operational trial by the Papua New Guinea National Weather Service. Its 54-concept knowledge base took 190 to 260 person-hours to build and 20 to 30 to review.
