AB  - With the development of deep generative models and large language models, highly realistic multi-modal disinformation is spreading and posing a severe threat. To address this challenge, the task of Detecting and Grounding Multi-Modal Media Manipulation (DGM4) has received attention from the academic community. However, most existing methods fail to explore the fine-grained topological structures and semantic consistency within local regions, leading to inadequate perception capabilities for hidden forgery traces. To this end, a Latent Graph-Guided Conflict Mining (LGCM) framework is proposed in this paper, aiming to enhance the fine-grained forgery perception capability in the DGM4 task. A latent graph-guided feature enhancement module is constructed in this framework, which maps low-level discrete features into a high-order entity space, thereby explicitly capturing the structured anti-forgery representations within modalities. Furthermore, to address the cross-modal semantic conflicts between images and texts, an attention-guided fine-grained conflict mining mechanism is designed during the deep interaction stage. This mechanism digs into abnormal forgery clues within consistent features and amplifies the cross-modal semantic conflicts of local forged regions through an asymmetric optimization strategy within the feature space. Experiments and visualization analyses on the DGM4 benchmark dataset demonstrate that the LGCM framework achieves optimal performance among baseline methods. Especially in the image manipulation region localization task, this method overcomes the masking effect of authentic backgrounds and improves localization precision.
