AB  - Machine learning offers a transformative path for optimization, shifting the paradigm from hand-crafted heuristics to learning-driven solvers. Yet this transition exposes a fundamental tension: learning-based methods generalize across instances but, in their unconstrained form, lack the verifiability that safety–critical deployment demands, whereas hand-crafted methods are reliable but resist adaptation. This tension sharpens in safety–critical domains such as agile earth observation satellite scheduling, where continuous-discrete coupling, time-dependent transition constraints, and multi-resource competition defeat both extremes working alone. This paper argues that the learn to optimize (L2O) paradigm offers a unifying meta-methodology for bridging this fracture, with large language models (LLMs) serving as semantically powerful yet unverified solver components within an L2O-governed framework. We support this argument through a critical synthesis of three previously disconnected research streams: L2O theory, LLM-assisted algorithm design, and satellite scheduling. From this synthesis, we identify five methodological fractures and distill three falsifiable scientific questions. These questions concern generalization under non-i.i.d. task distributions, convergence of distributed bilevel optimization under communication constraints, and formal verifiability of LLM-generated scheduling rules. Complementing this diagnosis, we propose a layered architecture in which an independent verification layer constrains both the L2O configuration process and the LLM semantic outputs. Rather than declaring a matured paradigm, this paper charts a research agenda toward verifiable, fault-tolerant, and distributed intelligent optimization for safety–critical aerospace systems, and outlines a concrete verification roadmap to guide the empirical validation of the proposed architecture.
