AB  - Post-disaster recovery of lifelines is critical, with numerous studies proposing tools for interdependent lifeline network recovery (LNR). However, existing tools themselves, whether standalone or embedded in systems, lack task-solving capabilities and still depend on human orchestration and execution for handling LNR tasks. Hence, this study proposes a development approach of large language model (LLM)-empowered agents that can intelligently utilize tools to solve LNR tasks. The approach comprises (i) the development of graph-guided LNR tools hosted on model context protocol (MCP) servers, (ii) the construction of LNR agents with the pattern of tool chain generation-tool execution (TCG-TE), and (iii) the evaluation and comparison of agents’ performance. The findings indicate that among the 32 compared agents, 5 out of 8 TCG-TE agents can successfully solve 50 LNR tasks, while none of the 24 traditional agents can solve all tasks. Agents following TCG-TE pattern with graph-guided MCP tools empowered by 8 LLMs solve 13.9% more tasks and reduce 68.2% of unnecessary tool invocations compared to the best-performing agents in traditional patterns. This study contributes by providing a development approach of LNR agents with task-solving capacities. Moreover, the proposed TCG-TE pattern alleviates agents’ difficulty in selecting and orchestrating tools.
