AB  - Kubernetes-orchestrated microservices facilitate scalable, low-latency IoT systems at the edge. However, such deployments remain susceptible to sophisticated cyberattacks. Although traditional Machine Learning (ML) classifiers achieve high detection accuracy, they often operate as opaque systems and lack the contextual information required for real-time event response in resource-constrained environments. This paper introduces a two-stage LLM-augmented intrusion detection system (IDS) tailored for edge-deployed IoT telemetry pipelines. The proposed architecture utilizes a lightweight gradient-boosted tree classifier (Stage 1) for real-time screening with sub-millisecond latency and selectively activates a Large Language Model (LLM) semantic analyzer (Stage 2) based on a confidence score established by the Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) team and derived from normalized entropy. Predictions exhibiting low confidence and uncertainty between competing classes are forwarded for in-depth semantic analysis. The adaptive, uncertainty-aware invocation strategy enables contextual attack classification, natural-language explanations, and Kubernetes-native remediation actions, while preserving edge efficiency. Evaluation on a drone feet dataset containing 128,782 records, encompassing both benign and attack classes, demonstrates that Stage 1 achieves an F1-score of 0.997. With a confidence threshold of 70%, only 4.16% of test samples are forwarded to Stage 2. These results suggest that integrating efficient ML with confidence-guided, on-demand LLM reasoning supports scalable, interpretable, and resource-aware intrusion detection at the edge without compromising performance.
