AB  - Serious Games (SGs) are widely used to support learning and behavior change; however, conventional designs often follow a static ”one-size-fits-all” approach, failing to adapt to player diversity. Adaptive Serious Games (ASGs) aim to deliver personalized interventions through player modeling. Yet, most existing systems rely on discriminative, modular architectures that retrieve feedback from predefined content libraries. This reliance causes “content bottlenecks”, increases architectural complexity and limits transferability across contexts. Current research primarily focuses on algorithmic performance or superficial design differences, lacking a systematic analysis of broader structural transitions in architecture, application domains, human-AI collaboration, and ethical challenges. To address this gap, we conducted a scoping review of 61 studies using the Context–Input–Process–Control framework. The results indicate early signs that AI-driven ASGs may be expanding from discriminative pipelines toward LLM-supported generative agents, although current LLM-based evidence remains limited. While this emerging shift reshapes human-AI collaboration, it also highlights a significant lag in safety and ethical governance. Building on these analyses, we propose the Collaborative Generative Adaptive System as a conceptual reference architecture that integrates multimodal LLM agents, human-in-the-loop mechanisms, and embedded ethical and safety design.
