AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) improves the factual grounding of LLMs through external evidence but remains vulnerable to hallucination when retrieval returns distractors. A common assumption in recent work is that Natural Language Inference (NLI) models can improve factuality by means of re-ranking the passages retrieved. We perform a controlled five-pipeline ablation on HotpotQA to isolate semantic (DPR), lexical (BM25), and factual (NLI) signals. We find that naive NLI-only re-ranking significantly degrades EM and F1; semantic and lexical signals are the dominant drivers of truthful answers. We propose an interpretable Advanced Hybrid reranker (DPR + BM25 + NLI) whose learned linear weights and empirical throughput make it a practical alternative to Cross-Encoders. We report accuracy, feature weights, and measured latency numbers in milliseconds per query for P1–P5 and include training and validation details so that others can verify our results. We also discuss the limitations, methodological choices, and deployment trade-offs. Our code and models are released to verify all claims made in this paper.
