AB  - Understanding and simulating the interaction between people and infrastructure is critical for planning and managing recovery after disasters. Human decision-making in the post-disaster context is often simulated using agent-based models, where household behavior is represented using decision rules. Such rules can be difficult to calibrate due to data scarcity and become intractable as the number of parameters that affect households increases. The aim of this paper is to replace rules with large language models (LLMs), transforming classical agents into generative agents in disaster recovery simulation. LLMs, whose training encompasses broad patterns of human behavior, can additionally be prompted with domain-specific scientific literature and decision rules to simulate how people behave after disasters, enabling simulation of complex decision-making. We show that the generative agent achieves 70% accuracy when compared to household displacement following disasters reported in the US Census Household Pulse Survey. Generative agents are then integrated with a regional recovery simulator of technical systems, in an effort to develop a predictive model of how households make decisions as the state of the built environment evolves during recovery. A case study considering post-earthquake recovery of buildings and the water supply system on the Alameda Island, CA, exposed to an earthquake scenario illustrates how generative agents could be used to forecast human behavior in a post-disaster context. We release an open-source implementation of the framework to facilitate validation of the regional socio-technical model as empirical data becomes available, acknowledging that the current work is preliminary.
