AB  - Multi-modal medical data, such as combining radiology images with pathology slides and clinical reports, are crucial for comprehensive diagnosis and treatment planning. Effectively integrating these heterogeneous sources remains a central challenge in medical AI. Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have demonstrated remarkable generalization by aligning text with images, and recent studies have explored their potential in medical applications. However, current VLM-based methods face two critical limitations: (1) the difficulty of effectively fusing multi-modal medical information, and (2) vulnerability to missing modalities that frequently occur in clinical practice. To address these challenges, we propose Multi-Instance CLIP (MI-CLIP), an extension of CLIP’s text-image framework explicitly designed for multi-modal medical data. MI-CLIP introduces two novel prompt learning strategies: Context-Guided Prompts facilitate the integration of multi-modal features by structuring the prompt around the semantic content of the textual query. This design guides the model to attend to relevant fused visual representations in a task-aware manner, thereby enhancing the extraction of clinically meaningful information from heterogeneous medical inputs. Modal-Guided Prompts align multi-modal medical data while maintaining robustness to missing modalities. Central to this design is a Prompt-Aware Attention mechanism, which models interactions among modality-specific prompts to dynamically exchange information and enhance the fusion of incomplete inputs. Our extensive experiments on multiple medical multi-modal datasets show that MI-CLIP significantly enhances the performance of medical image fusion, both in multi-modal and missing modality scenarios, demonstrating its ability to tackle real-world challenges.
