AB  - Urban resilience is a knowledge-intensive field in which domain knowledge is distributed across academic literature, technical standards, policy documents, engineering reports, and disaster case records. General-purpose large language models (LLMs) often provide insufficient coverage of resilience-specific terminology, infrastructure mechanisms, engineering constraints, and decision-oriented tasks. This study presents ResCity-LLM, an end-to-end framework for developing and evaluating locally deployable LLMs for urban resilience applications. A multi-source ResCity-Corpus is first constructed, and a multi-agent, dual-stage data generation pipeline is developed to transform heterogeneous documents into continued pre-training (CPT) and supervised fine-tuning (SFT) datasets. Multiple open-source model families are then adapted through LoRA-based CPT and SFT and evaluated using ResCity-Eval, a domain benchmark covering knowledge question answering, mechanism explanation, structured analysis, and strategy and pathway generation. The training curves indicate stable convergence across the evaluated model families. On ResCity-Eval, Qwen2.5-14B-SFT achieves the highest overall weighted score of 0.683, exceeding Qwen-max and DeepSeek-v4-flash by 0.075 and 0.099, respectively. Task-level comparisons and ablation experiments further show that the effects of CPT and SFT vary across model families and are not uniformly beneficial. A knowledge-service demonstration and an urban seismic resilience agent case study show that ResCity-LLM can support local domain knowledge services and function as the language component of professional agents. The proposed framework provides a reproducible pathway for corpus construction, model adaptation, domain-specific evaluation, and agent-oriented application of LLMs in urban resilience.
